-- je54-seat: the questions a reader of an enactment asks of the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, as exported functions.  The seat is the reader of an enactment: someone who has to know what an enactment means for them,
-- when it bound them, what may be done to them, and whether a notice reached them in time.  This module covers the text of the Law, Article 1 (commencement), Articles 2 to 5 and the Schedule (words), Article 7
-- (service by post) and Article 8 (bankruptcy); je54-seat-powers-and-penalties covers Articles 9 to 15 and the Articles that are in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 only (6, 16, 17).
-- Every export takes the day the question is about and reads the text of the Law in force on that day, except where the comment above it says which other day selects the text.  Where the Law leaves a point open the
-- export refuses with the reason (that is the answer, not an error); the readings chosen at forks I1 to I6 are inputs with their defaults stated (NOTES.md section 6).
-- "Comes into operation" (Article 1(4)) and "comes into force" are treated as the same event (assumed, not ruled); the names say "come into force".

§ `Which text of the Law`

-- Selecting date: the day given.

-- src:187 | Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021
-- src:188 | L.8/2021
-- src:189 | 28 September 2021 (R&O.112/2021)
-- src:190 | P.26/2021
-- src:206 | Companies (Jersey) Amendment No. 2 Law 2026
-- src:207 | L.33/2026
-- src:208 | 19 June 2026
-- src:209 | P.49/2026
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Endnotes, Table of Legislation History, rows L.8/2021 and L.33/2026, and the other rows that the vintage table cites (src:187-190), (src:206-209)
@export Find which text of the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954 was in force on a given day: one of the seven consolidated texts held, the inferred text for 28 March 2003 to 31 December 2018, or no text before 28 March 2003
GIVEN
    d IS A DATE @desc The day, as year-month-day
GIVETH A `Text of the Law`
`which text of the Law was in force on a day` d MEANS
    `the text of the Law in force on` d

§ `Article 1: when an enactment comes into force`

-- Selecting date: fork I1 (the `reading` input); by default the enactment's own commencement decides whether the Law's Articles 1(5) and 1(6) or the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021 govern.

-- src:8 | (4) In this Law and in every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the expression “commencement”, when used with reference to an enactment, shall mean the time at which the enactment comes into operation.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:9 | (5) Where an enactment is expressed or construed as being expressed to come into operation on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into operation immediately on the expiration of the previous day.[1]
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:10 | (6) Where an enactment, whenever passed or made, is expressed to come into operation forthwith or is silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation, the enactment shall be construed as being expressed to come into operation on the day on which it was passed or made.[2]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(4) (src:8) and Article 1(5) and 1(6) as they read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:9-10)
@export Find the day on which an enactment came into force under the Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Articles 1(5) and 1(6), for an enactment made before 28 September 2021; later enactments are governed by the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021 and the answer refuses
GIVEN
    c IS A `Commencement as stated` @desc How the enactment states when it is to come into force, the day it names if any, the day it was passed or made, and (only for the last reading of fork I1) the day on which you are asking
    reading IS A `Which day selects the text for commencement` TYPICALLY `whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021` @desc Which day selects the text of the Law read for the commencement of the enactment (fork I1): whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021 (the default), the day it was passed or made, or the day on which the question about it is asked
GIVETH A `Day with its basis`
`on what day did the enactment come into force` c reading MEANS
    `Article 1(5) and 1(6) by date: the day on which the enactment comes into force` c reading

-- src:8 | (4) In this Law and in every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the expression “commencement”, when used with reference to an enactment, shall mean the time at which the enactment comes into operation.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:9 | (5) Where an enactment is expressed or construed as being expressed to come into operation on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into operation immediately on the expiration of the previous day.[1]
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:10 | (6) Where an enactment, whenever passed or made, is expressed to come into operation forthwith or is silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation, the enactment shall be construed as being expressed to come into operation on the day on which it was passed or made.[2]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(4) (src:8) and Article 1(5) and 1(6) as they read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:9-10)
@export Find the moment at which an enactment came into force, which is the start of the day on which it comes into force, on the clock of the time zone given
GIVEN
    c IS A `Commencement as stated` @desc How the enactment states when it is to come into force, the day it names if any, the day it was passed or made, and (only for the last reading of fork I1) the day on which you are asking
    zone IS A STRING @desc The time zone in which the day is read, as a tz database name, for example Europe/Jersey; the Law names no zone, and Jersey local time is the usual assumption
    reading IS A `Which day selects the text for commencement` TYPICALLY `whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021` @desc Which day selects the text of the Law read for the commencement of the enactment (fork I1): whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021 (the default), the day it was passed or made, or the day on which the question about it is asked
GIVETH A `Moment with its basis`
`at what moment did the enactment come into force` c zone reading MEANS
    `Article 1(4) by date: the moment at which the enactment comes into force` c zone reading

-- src:8 | (4) In this Law and in every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the expression “commencement”, when used with reference to an enactment, shall mean the time at which the enactment comes into operation.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:9 | (5) Where an enactment is expressed or construed as being expressed to come into operation on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into operation immediately on the expiration of the previous day.[1]
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:10 | (6) Where an enactment, whenever passed or made, is expressed to come into operation forthwith or is silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation, the enactment shall be construed as being expressed to come into operation on the day on which it was passed or made.[2]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(4) (src:8) and Article 1(5) and 1(6) as they read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:9-10)
@export Say whether an enactment was in force on a given day, an enactment being in force from the start of the day on which it comes into force
GIVEN
    d IS A DATE @desc The day to test, as year-month-day
    c IS A `Commencement as stated` @desc How the enactment states when it is to come into force, the day it names if any, the day it was passed or made, and (only for the last reading of fork I1) the day on which you are asking
    reading IS A `Which day selects the text for commencement` TYPICALLY `whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021` @desc Which day selects the text of the Law read for the commencement of the enactment (fork I1): whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021 (the default), the day it was passed or made, or the day on which the question about it is asked
GIVETH A `Yes or no with its basis`
`was the enactment in force on a day` d c reading MEANS
    `Article 1(4) by date: the enactment is in force on the day` d c reading

-- src:8 | (4) In this Law and in every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the expression “commencement”, when used with reference to an enactment, shall mean the time at which the enactment comes into operation.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:9 | (5) Where an enactment is expressed or construed as being expressed to come into operation on a particular day, it shall be construed as coming into operation immediately on the expiration of the previous day.[1]
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:10 | (6) Where an enactment, whenever passed or made, is expressed to come into operation forthwith or is silent as to the time at which it is to come into operation, the enactment shall be construed as being expressed to come into operation on the day on which it was passed or made.[2]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(4) (src:8) and Article 1(5) and 1(6) as they read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:9-10)
@export Say whether an enactment was in force at a given moment, an enactment being in force from the start of the day on which it comes into force on the clock of the time zone given
GIVEN
    t IS A DATETIME @desc The moment to test, with its time zone, in ISO 8601 form
    zone IS A STRING @desc The time zone in which the day is read, as a tz database name, for example Europe/Jersey; the Law names no zone, and Jersey local time is the usual assumption
    c IS A `Commencement as stated` @desc How the enactment states when it is to come into force, the day it names if any, the day it was passed or made, and (only for the last reading of fork I1) the day on which you are asking
    reading IS A `Which day selects the text for commencement` TYPICALLY `whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021` @desc Which day selects the text of the Law read for the commencement of the enactment (fork I1): whether the enactment had come into force before 28 September 2021 (the default), the day it was passed or made, or the day on which the question about it is asked
GIVETH A `Yes or no with its basis`
`was the enactment in force at a moment` t zone c reading MEANS
    `Article 1(4) by date: the enactment is in force at the instant` t zone c reading

-- Selecting date: the day the question is asked about, for Articles 1(1), 1(2) and 1(3); the day the enactment was passed or made, for Article 1(7).

-- src:5 | (1) In this Law and in every other enactment (as hereby defined) whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the expression “enactment”, unless a contrary intention appears, shall mean any provision of any Law passed by the States and confirmed by Order in Council and any provision of any regulations, Order, rules, bye-laws, scheme or other instrument passed or made in Jersey under the authority of any Order in Council or under any such Law as aforesaid.[1]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(1) (src:5)
@export Say whether an instrument is an enactment under Article 1(1): a Law passed by the States and confirmed in Council, or regulations, an Order, rules, bye-laws or a scheme passed or made under a Law or under the authority of an Order in Council
GIVEN
    `the day the question is asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you are asking, as year-month-day
    k IS A `Kind of instrument` @desc The kind of instrument
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 1(1) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the instrument an enactment` `the day the question is asked about` k `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 1(1) on a day: the instrument is an enactment` `the day the question is asked about` k `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:6 | (2) For the purposes of this Law and of every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, unless a contrary intention appears, a Law shall not be deemed to have been passed until it has been confirmed by Her Majesty in Council and registered by the Royal Court.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(2), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:6)
@export Find the day on which a Law is deemed to have been passed under Article 1(2), which is in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 only: not until it has been confirmed in Council and registered by the Royal Court
GIVEN
    `the day the question is asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you are asking, as year-month-day
    `the day it was confirmed by Her Majesty in Council` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the Law was confirmed in Council
    `the day it was registered by the Royal Court` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the Law was registered by the Royal Court
    reading IS A `Reading of Article 1(2)` TYPICALLY `the Law is deemed passed on the later of the day it was confirmed and the day it was registered` @desc Fork I3: whether the Article fixes the day as the later of the two days (the default) or only bars an earlier day, in which case you state the day
    `the day the caller states` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The day you say the Law is deemed passed; read only on the second reading of fork I3; leave empty otherwise
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 1(2) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A DATE
`on what day is a Law deemed to have been passed` `the day the question is asked about` `the day it was confirmed by Her Majesty in Council` `the day it was registered by the Royal Court` reading `the day the caller states` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 1(2) on a day: the day on which a Law is deemed passed` `the day the question is asked about` `the day it was confirmed by Her Majesty in Council` `the day it was registered by the Royal Court` reading `the day the caller states` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:7 | (3) In this Law and in every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, references to the passing or repeal of an enactment shall be construed, where appropriate, as references to the making or rescission of that enactment.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(3) (src:7)
@export Find how a reference to the passing or the repeal of an enactment is read under Article 1(3): as a reference to its making or its rescission, where it is appropriate to read it so
GIVEN
    `the day the question is asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you are asking, as year-month-day
    word IS A `Word read by Article 1(3)` @desc The word in the enactment: passing or repeal
    `it is appropriate to read it so` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is appropriate, in the enactment concerned, to read the word as making or rescission
GIVETH A `Reading under Article 1(3)`
`how is a reference to passing or repeal read` `the day the question is asked about` word `it is appropriate to read it so` MEANS
    `Article 1(3) on a day: how a reference to passing or repeal is read` `the day the question is asked about` word `it is appropriate to read it so`

-- src:11 | (7) Where the time at which an enactment, whenever passed or made, is to come into operation is expressed or calculated by reference to its promulgation, and the date of promulgation is unknown, that date shall be deemed to be the day the enactment was passed or made.[5]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 1(7) (src:11)
@export Find the date of promulgation to use under Article 1(7) where the time at which an enactment comes into force is expressed or calculated by reference to its promulgation
GIVEN
    `the time is expressed or calculated by reference to promulgation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment expresses or calculates the time by reference to its promulgation
    `the date of promulgation` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The date of promulgation, if known; leave empty if unknown
    `the day the enactment was passed or made` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the enactment was passed or made
GIVETH A DATE
`which date of promulgation is to be used` `the time is expressed or calculated by reference to promulgation` `the date of promulgation` `the day the enactment was passed or made` MEANS
    `Article 1(7) by date: the date of promulgation to be used` `the time is expressed or calculated by reference to promulgation` `the date of promulgation` `the day the enactment was passed or made`

§ `Articles 2, 3, 4 and 5 and the Schedule: what words mean`

-- Selecting date: the day the question is about.  Articles 2 to 5 refuse for a day before 1 January 2019; the Schedule's "midnight" and "month" are answered from 28 March 2003 by inference.

-- src:13 | In this Law and in every other enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, unless the contrary intention appears –
-- src:14 | (a) words importing the masculine gender shall include females;
-- src:15 | (b) words in the singular shall include the plural and words in the plural shall include the singular; and
-- src:16 | (c) grammatical variations of words to which definitions are assigned shall be construed in accordance with the definitions.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 2 (src:13-16)
@export Find how a word of an enactment is read under Article 2: words importing the masculine gender include females, the singular includes the plural and the plural the singular, and grammatical variations of defined words follow the definition
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    kind IS A `Kind of word under Article 2` @desc Which kind of word is being read
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 2 allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A `Reach of a word under Article 2`
`how is the word of the enactment read` `the day the question is about` kind `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 2: how a word is read` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) kind `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:18 | In the construction of every enactment relating to a punishable offence, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the expression “person” shall, unless the contrary intention appears, include a body corporate:
-- src:19 | Provided that nothing in this Article shall render any body corporate liable, in respect of any act or omission occurring before the date of the commencement of this Law, to any criminal proceedings to which that body corporate would not have been liable before that date.
-- src:108 | “person” shall include any body of persons corporate or unincorporated;
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 3 (src:18-19) and the Schedule, Part 1, definition of person (src:108)
@export Find whether the word person in an enactment includes a body corporate or an unincorporated body, under Article 3 and the Schedule definition of person, with the proviso protecting a body from liability for what it did before 21 August 1954
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    kind IS A `Kind of body` @desc Whether the body is a body corporate or an unincorporated body
    `the enactment relates to a punishable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment relates to a punishable offence
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 3 allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
    `the day of the act or omission` IS A DATE @desc The day of the act or omission complained of
    `the body corporate would have been liable to the proceedings before that date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body would have been liable to the proceedings if the act or omission had happened before 21 August 1954
    reading IS A `Reach of the proviso to Article 3` TYPICALLY `the proviso limits Article 3 only` @desc Fork I5: whether the proviso limits Article 3 only (the default) or also limits the Schedule definition of person
GIVETH A `How person reaches a body`
`does person include this body` `the day the question is about` kind `the enactment relates to a punishable offence` `a contrary intention appears` `the day of the act or omission` `the body corporate would have been liable to the proceedings before that date` reading MEANS
    `how person reaches the body` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) kind `the enactment relates to a punishable offence` `a contrary intention appears` `the day of the act or omission` `the body corporate would have been liable to the proceedings before that date` reading

-- src:21 | (1) The definitions in Part 1 of the Schedule shall, unless the contrary intention appears, apply to every enactment, whenever passed or made.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 4(1) and the Schedule, Part 1 (src:21)
@export Find what a Part 1 definition of the Schedule does for a thing asked about: whether the thing is within the word, outside it, left to the word's own meaning, or the definition does not apply because its condition is not met
GIVEN
    `the word` IS A STRING @desc The defined word, exactly as the Schedule prints it, for example pharmacist
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    named IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the definition names the thing asked about
    `the condition is met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the condition the definition states for itself is met; true if it states none
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 4(1) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
    reading IS A `Reading of a definition that both means and includes` TYPICALLY `the named things are all that the word covers` @desc Fork I6: for the one definition that both means and includes (Magistrate), whether the named things are all that the word covers (the default) or do not exhaust it
GIVETH A `Reach of a defined expression`
`what does the Schedule definition do for the word` `the word` `the day the question is about` named `the condition is met` `a contrary intention appears` reading MEANS
    `Article 4(1): how a word defined in Part 1 of the Schedule reaches a thing` `the word` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) named `the condition is met` `a contrary intention appears` reading

-- src:103 | “midnight”, in relation to a day, shall mean the last moment of that day;
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Schedule, Part 1, definition of midnight (src:103)
@export Say whether a thing done at a moment was done by midnight on a given day, midnight in relation to a day being the last moment of that day, on the clock of the zone in which the days are read
GIVEN
    d IS A DATE @desc The day by whose midnight the thing had to be done
    m IS A `Local moment` @desc The moment at which the thing was done, on the same clock
GIVETH A `Yes or no with its basis`
`was the thing done by midnight on the day` d m MEANS
    `the thing was done by midnight on` d `being` m `in the text` (`the text of the Law in force on` (m's `calendar day`))

-- src:24 | In an enactment, unless the context otherwise requires, the name commonly applied to a country, place, body, administration, corporation, society, officer, functionary, person, party or thing shall mean the country, place, body, administration, corporation, society, officer, functionary, person, party or thing to which the name is commonly applied, whether or not the name is the formal or unabbreviated designation thereof.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 5 (src:24)
@export Find what a commonly used name means in an enactment under Article 5: the name commonly applied to a country, place, body, administration, corporation, society, officer, functionary, person, party or thing means that thing, unless the context otherwise requires
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the thing to which the name is commonly applied` IS A STRING @desc The thing to which the name is commonly applied
    `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the context of the enactment requires the name to be read otherwise
GIVETH A STRING
`what does the commonly used name mean` `the day the question is about` `the thing to which the name is commonly applied` `the context otherwise requires` MEANS
    `Article 5: what a commonly used name means` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the thing to which the name is commonly applied` `the context otherwise requires`

§ `Article 7: service by post`

-- Selecting date: the day the letter was posted.

-- src:27 | Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, authorizes or requires any document to be served by post, whether the expression “serve”, “give” or “send”, or any other expression is used, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the service shall be deemed to be effected by properly addressing, prepaying and posting a letter containing the document, and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 7 (src:27)
@export Say whether service of a document by post is deemed effected under Article 7: the enactment authorizes or requires service by post and the letter was properly addressed, prepaid and posted, unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the letter was posted` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the letter was posted, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read
    s IS A `Service by post as it happened` @desc What happened to the document: whether an enactment authorizes or requires service by post, whether the letter was properly addressed, prepaid and posted, the time at which it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post, and any other time proved
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is service by post deemed effected` `the day the letter was posted` s `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 7: service by post is deemed effected` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the letter was posted`) s `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:27 | Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, authorizes or requires any document to be served by post, whether the expression “serve”, “give” or “send”, or any other expression is used, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the service shall be deemed to be effected by properly addressing, prepaying and posting a letter containing the document, and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 7 (src:27)
@export Find the time at which service of a document by post is deemed effected under Article 7: the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post, unless the contrary is proved
GIVEN
    `the day the letter was posted` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the letter was posted, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read
    s IS A `Service by post as it happened` @desc What happened to the document: whether an enactment authorizes or requires service by post, whether the letter was properly addressed, prepaid and posted, the time at which it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post, and any other time proved
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned
    reading IS A `What governs once the presumption of Article 7 is rebutted` TYPICALLY `the time that is proved` @desc Fork I4: once the contrary is proved, whether the time proved governs (the default) or the Article gives no time
GIVETH A `Local moment`
`at what time is service by post deemed effected` `the day the letter was posted` s `a contrary intention appears` reading MEANS
    `Article 7: the time at which service by post is deemed effected` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the letter was posted`) s `a contrary intention appears` reading

-- src:27 | Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, authorizes or requires any document to be served by post, whether the expression “serve”, “give” or “send”, or any other expression is used, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the service shall be deemed to be effected by properly addressing, prepaying and posting a letter containing the document, and, unless the contrary is proved, to have been effected at the time at which the letter would be delivered in the ordinary course of post.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 7 (src:27)
@export Find the day on which service of a document by post is deemed effected under Article 7, the day of the time at which it is deemed effected
GIVEN
    `the day the letter was posted` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the letter was posted, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read
    s IS A `Service by post as it happened` @desc What happened to the document: whether an enactment authorizes or requires service by post, whether the letter was properly addressed, prepaid and posted, the time at which it would be delivered in the ordinary course of post, and any other time proved
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned
    reading IS A `What governs once the presumption of Article 7 is rebutted` TYPICALLY `the time that is proved` @desc Fork I4: once the contrary is proved, whether the time proved governs (the default) or the Article gives no time
GIVETH A DATE
`on what day is service by post deemed effected` `the day the letter was posted` s `a contrary intention appears` reading MEANS
    `Article 7: the day on which service by post is deemed effected` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the letter was posted`) s `a contrary intention appears` reading

§ `Article 8: bankruptcy`

-- Selecting date: fork I2 (the `selecting` input): by default the day of the event.

-- src:29 | In every enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, references to a person becoming bankrupt shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be construed as references to either –
-- src:30 | (a) the grant by the Royal Court of an application made by that person to place the person’s property under the control of the Court (de remettre ses biens entre les mains de la Justice);
-- src:31 | (b) the declaring of the property of that person to be en désastre;
-- src:32 | (ba) the grant by the Viscount of a debt remission order in respect of that person’s qualifying debts under the Debt Remission (Individuals) (Jersey) Law 2016;
-- src:33 | (c) the grant by the Royal Court of an application made by that person to make a general cession of the person’s property (de faire cession générale de tous ses biens-meubles et héritages);
-- src:34 | (d) a decision of the Royal Court adjudging the property of that person to be renounced (adjugée renoncée);
-- src:35 | (e) in the case of a company, the winding up of the company by means of a creditors’ winding up pursuant to the Companies (Jersey) Law 1991;
-- src:36 | (ea) in the case of a company, the making of an administration order under Part 20B of the Companies (Jersey) Law 1991;
-- src:37 | (f) in the case of a limited liability company, the winding up of a limited liability company by means of a creditors’ winding up under the Limited Liability Companies (Winding Up and Dissolution) (Jersey) Regulations 2022; or
-- src:38 | (g) in the case of a bank, the making of a bank winding up order under the Bank (Recovery and Resolution) (Jersey) Law 2017,
-- src:39 | and references to bankruptcy shall be construed accordingly.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 8 (src:29-39)
@export Say whether an event is a person becoming bankrupt for the purposes of any enactment, under Article 8: the listed events of the text read, which grew in 2022 and 2026
GIVEN
    e IS AN `Event in the life of a person` @desc The event
    person IS A `Person who may have become bankrupt` @desc Whether the person is a company, a limited liability company or a bank
    `the day on which the event happened` IS A DATE @desc The day of the event, as year-month-day
    `the day on which the question is asked` IS A DATE @desc The day on which you are asking, as year-month-day
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned
    selecting IS A `Which day selects the text for Article 8` TYPICALLY `the day of the event` @desc Fork I2: whether the text in force on the day of the event (the default) or on the day the question is asked governs
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the event a person becoming bankrupt` e person `the day on which the event happened` `the day on which the question is asked` `a contrary intention appears` selecting MEANS
    `Article 8: on the day of the event and the day of the question, the event is a person becoming bankrupt` e person `the day on which the event happened` `the day on which the question is asked` `a contrary intention appears` selecting
